  "preamble": "The transformation of the voiceless velar plosive /k/ to the voiced velar plosive /g/ is a common phonetic shift, primarily driven by processes of voicing assimilation or lenition. This change often occurs in specific phonological environments, such as intervocalically, after nasals, or in morphologically conditioned contexts. Kichwa and Cajamarca Quechua voice *k after a nasal (*ʈʂunka > ʧuŋga 'ten').",
